    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "thanks. one thing we're worried about with the high traffic is the aesthetic degradation over time. specifically, we want to avoid the floor looking \"beaten up\" or having lots of tiny random scuff marks and scratches from people's shoes and cleaning equipment within the first year. between those three, which one hides surface damage/wear the best?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Natural stone, particularly materials with a busy, complex veining pattern like certain granites, is best at disguising minor scuffs and surface wear. Polished concrete can also appear quite monolithic and forgiving, especially if it has a slightly textured or aggregate finish. LVT generally shows scratches and scuff marks more prominently because of its smooth, uniform surface unless a very high-quality, textured pattern is chosen.",
      "source": "target",
